za-Michael addition of ammonia to acrylonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "mildly basic ammonia-bearing pools"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds across the activated C=C bond of acrylonitrile to give beta-aminopropionitrile.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) explicitly used ammonia addition to acrylonitrile to access beta-aminopropionitrile, a key branch-point precursor for proline. No special mineral catalyst is required; the chemistry is a straightforward aza-Michael addition favored in NH3-bearing mildly basic waters (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "UV/H2S homologation and cyclization of beta-aminopropionitrile to gamma-butyrothiolactam", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation; laboratory analog uses 254 nm; sulfide-rich shallow water; net sequence passes through aldehyde, cyanohydrin, and hydroxythioamide intermediates"}, "mechanism": "Net cyanosulfidic sequence: nitrile photoreduction to the aldehyde, HCN addition to the cyanohydrin, sulfide conversion to thioamide chemistry, then photodeoxygenation and ring closure to the cyclic thioamide.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) resolved the downstream nitrile-to-aldehyde, cyanohydrin, thioamide, and cyclization chemistry leading to gamma-butyrothiolactam. This node compresses those observed substeps into one net transformation to keep the network within the requested size. H2S can act as the stoichiometric photoreductant without Cu photoredox acceleration, but the need for sulfidic anions and laboratory 254 nm UV remains a major environmental critique (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Cu-photoredox-assisted UV/H2S homologation and cyclization of beta-aminopropionitrile to gamma-butyrothiolactam", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation; laboratory analog uses 254 nm; HS-/H2S serves as reductant while copper accelerates the photoredox sequence"}, "mechanism": "The same net cyanosulfidic homologation as rxn_003 occurs, but Cu(I)/Cu(II) mediates faster photoredox turnover during reduction and cyclization.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) reported that Cu(I)-Cu(II) photoredox can accelerate the cyanosulfidic network. It is treated here as a distinct pathway because copper changes the redox mediation while preserving the same net chemistry. Cu scarcity and sulfide complexation mean this version is plausible only in localized Cu-bearing surface settings (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Photoreductive conversion of gamma-butyrothiolactam to proline aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation; inferred cyclic imine intermediate; HCN capture furnishes the alpha-aminonitrile"}, "mechanism": "Further sulfide-driven photoreduction and desulfurization generates a cyclic imine that undergoes Strecker-type HCN addition to give pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) reported conversion of the cyclic thioamide to proline alpha-aminonitrile in 32% over the final two steps; the intervening cyclic imine was inferred rather than isolated. Ritson (2021) noted that proline aminonitrile is unusually resistant to retro-Strecker reversion relative to acyclic aminonitriles, making this product especially durable under fluctuating NH3 or pH (Patel et al., 2015; Ritson, 2021)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Hydrolysis of proline aminonitrile to DL-proline", "inputs": ["mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "prolonged aqueous hydrolysis during wet-dry or geothermal cycling; likely proceeds through an amide intermediate"}, "mechanism": "Stepwise nitrile hydration and hydrolysis converts the aminonitrile into the amino acid carboxylate.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) stopped at the aminonitrile and did not report a proline hydrolysis yield, so this is chemically expected but quantitatively unverified for that system. It is retained because aminonitrile hydrolysis is the canonical route from Strecker products to amino acids, and proline aminonitrile is comparatively persistent rather than strongly retroconverting (Patel et al., 2015; Ritson, 2021)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Strecker-type capture of 4-aminobutanal to proline aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "electric discharge not modeled explicitly; 4-aminobutanal represents a discharge-derived precursor pool and cyclizes to 1-pyrroline in water"}, "mechanism": "4-Aminobutanal cyclizes to 1-pyrroline, which then undergoes HCN addition to yield pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile.", "reasoning": "Ring et al. (1972) reported proline in Miller-type discharge products, but no 4-aminobutanal or proline-aminonitrile sequence was isolated. This step is therefore an inferred mechanistic reconstruction from general Strecker logic rather than a directly demonstrated prebiotic transformation. Parker et al. (2011) also emphasized that common OPA/NAC analyses miss secondary amines such as proline, so later non-detections are not decisive (Ring et al., 1972; Parker et al., 2011)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Activated reduction and cyclization of glutamate to pyrroline-5-carboxylate", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["FeS", "Ni"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-380K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "FeS/Ni surfaces", "aqueous": true, "other": "alkaline H2-rich vent or subseafloor setting; gamma-carboxylate activation is required before reduction; spontaneous cyclization of glutamate-5-semialdehyde is implicit"}, "mechanism": "A speculative surface-mediated activation of the gamma-carboxylate is followed by reduction to glutamate-5-semialdehyde, which then cyclizes nearly completely to P5C.", "reasoning": "Hydrothermal and metal-mediated reductive amination can generate glutamate from alpha-ketoglutarate, but the glutamate -> GSA/P5C step has no prebiotic demonstration and is the main bottleneck of the metabolism-first route. It is included as a chemically motivated but explicitly speculative step because biology spends energy to activate this carboxylate, whereas prebiotic analogs remain unknown; once GSA forms, cyclization to P5C is expected to be facile (Huber & Wachtershauser, 2003; Kitadai et al., 2019; Kaur et al., 2024; Smith & Greenberg, 1956; Fichman et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Reduction of pyrroline-5-carboxylate to DL-proline", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Ni", "FeS"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Ni/FeS surfaces", "aqueous": true, "other": "moderate-temperature alkaline vent chemistry; proposed metal-mediated imine reduction under H2-rich conditions"}, "mechanism": "Metal-assisted reduction of the cyclic imine in P5C yields racemic proline.", "reasoning": "P5C reduction is chemically easier than the preceding glutamate carboxylate reduction, and related pyrroline carboxylates are readily reduced in the laboratory, but no prebiotic H2/Ni, Fe0, or FeS example has been reported for proline. This node is therefore speculative but a priority experimental target for protometabolic frameworks (Smith & Greenberg, 1956; Fichman et al., 2015; Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydrothermal conversion of arginine to DL-proline", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "370-410K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "subseafloor hydrothermal pyrolysis at moderate temperatures to limit amino-acid destruction; ornithine and pyrroline-like intermediates are implicit"}, "mechanism": "Net deguanidination, deamination, and intramolecular cyclization through ornithine-like and pyrroline-like intermediates gives proline.", "reasoning": "Vallentyne (1968) reported proline among aqueous arginine pyrolysis products, making this the only nonenzymatic hint of an ornithine or arginine branch to proline. The mechanism and even the historical product assignment remain uncertain, and modern re-analysis has been recommended; moreover arginine itself is a difficult prebiotic feedstock, so this branch is included only as a weak, testable alternative (Vallentyne, 1968; Korner et al., 2021)."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Eutectic phase enrichment of DL-proline to L-proline", "inputs": ["mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "280-310K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "requires a small initial L-bias; node represents the L-enriched recoverable fraction from dissolution-crystallization cycling"}, "mechanism": "Preferential crystallization and solid-solution partitioning enrich the recoverable liquid or dissolved fraction in the L-enantiomer while racemate remains in the solid phase.", "reasoning": "No abiotic synthesis route yields L-proline directly, so a separate chiral amplification step is required. Proline is the best-studied amino acid for physical amplification: Klussmann et al. (2006) showed that solution ee is pinned near the eutectic composition, reaching about 54-58% ee in methanol or ethanol systems. The more dramatic CHCl3 cosolvate case is not prebiotic, so this network retains only the more geochemically defensible eutectic principle and treats the required seed ee as an environmental condition rather than a separate node (Klussmann et al., 2006; Blackmond, 2019)."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Sequential phase-behavior amplification of DL-proline to L-proline", "inputs": ["mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "280-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "requires a small initial L-bias; repeated dissolution and recrystallization across phase boundaries under geothermal cycling"}, "mechanism": "Serial partial dissolution and recrystallization under changing phase conditions ratchets a small L-excess upward in the recoverable fraction.", "reasoning": "This second chirality route is more speculative than the direct eutectic step, but it is chemically motivated by modeled sequential amplification across changing conglomerate and racemic-compound regimes. It is included because direct asymmetric proline synthesis is unknown, and cyclic physical enrichment is geochemically more plausible than the nonprebiotic CHCl3 cosolvate system even though it remains untested specifically for proline (Sharma, 2023; Blackmond, 2019)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Minimal-copper cyanosulfidic route plus eutectic enrichment", "description": "Patel-type surface synthesis with Cu(I) used for HCN-acetylene coupling, H2S-only downstream photochemistry, then moderate physical enrichment to L-proline.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_006