action as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.44\n- Feedback: The network is well framed around the correct literature backbone: the Patel cyanosulfidic route is rightly central, and it is good that meteorite and ice detections were not promoted to mechanistic terrestrial pathways. The graph is connected and uses consistent molecule IDs. However network validity is substantially weakened because 8 of 12 reaction nodes are ground-truth UNBALANCED: rxn_003, rxn_004, rxn_005, rxn_006, rxn_007, rxn_008, rxn_009, and rxn_010. Specific fixes are available for some nodes: rxn_007 needs H2O as product; rxn_009 needs H2 as reactant; rxn_006 should be written as mol_008 + 2 H2O -> mol_013 + NH3; rxn_010 needs explicit coproducts for the lost guanidino fragment, with guanidine being one plausible net coproduct. rxn_005 may need sulfur coproducts or H2S moved from reactants to conditions; a possible shorthand is mol_007 + mol_001 -> mol_008 + S. rxn_003 and rxn_004 cannot be rescued by a simple small-species patch and should be expanded into Patel's explicit aldehyde, cyanohydrin, thioamide, thiolactam, and imine-level substeps. rxn_008 also needs explicit activation and reductant chemistry, not just a one-arrow hydrothermal conversion. The hydrothermal glutamate or P5C and arginine branches are much less supported than the cyanosulfidic branch and should be marked as hypotheses rather than parallel validated routes. Finally, rxn_011 and especially rxn_012 are not full synthetic conversions to L-proline; proline phase behavior can enrich ee only with a seed ee, explicit solvent or eutectic conditions, and a modeled D-enriched companion phase, and the Biochemical environment label should be replaced by explicit prebiotic pool, eutectic, freeze-thaw, or evaporation-crystallization settings. Overall, the network is coherent in concept and literature-aware, but it is not currently a chemically valid or fully target-achieving synthesis network for L-proline.\n- Issues: ['P1 is the strongest and most literature-aligned branch because rxn_001 and rxn_002 match the Patel cyanosulfidic entry to the proline skeleton. The explicit network then breaks conservation at the core: rxn_003 and rxn_005 are ground-truth UNBALANCED bundled transformations, and rxn_006 is UNBALANCED and should include 2 H2O as reactants and NH3 as product. Literature support is also strongest only up to mol_008; the aminonitrile hydrolysis to proline is not quantified here. rxn_011 reflects real proline phase behavior, but it is a resolution step rather than a full DL to L conversion and needs a seed ee plus an explicit D-rich companion phase. Good route to proline aminonitrile; not yet a validated route to isolated L-proline.', 'P2 keeps the same credible early cyanosulfidic backbone as P1, but rxn_003, rxn_005, and rxn_006 remain unbalanced and therefore invalid as written. The final step is weaker than in P1: rxn_012 is a speculative sequential amplification module rather than an experimentally established prebiotic resolution of proline. As drawn, this path does not convincingly deliver L-proline beyond a hypothetical enrichment scenario.', 'P3 follows the Cu-assisted Patel variant and is reasonable on stated conditions, but Cu acceleration does not fix the reaction specification. rxn_004 and rxn_005 are ground-truth UNBALANCED bundled nets, rxn_006 should include 2 H2O and NH3, and the literature support still weakens after mol_008 because hydrolysis to proline is unquantified. rxn_011 again represents enrichment or resolution behavior, not a complete synthetic conversion to L-proline. This remains a partial route to racemic proline precursors rather than a validated route to L-proline.', 'P4 inherits the same chemically sensible entry from Patel chemistry as P3, but the explicit core still violates conservation in rxn_004 and rxn_005, and rxn_006 still omits the water and ammonia required for hydrolysis. The final rxn_012 step is even less secure than rxn_011 because sequential phase-behavior amplification is not demonstrated here for proline under prebiotic conditions. The route is coherent as a hypothesis, not as a validated synthesis to L-proline.', 'P5 is chemically conceivable as an inferred Strecker capture of 4-aminobutanal or 1-pyrroline, and rxn_007 is close to correct if H2O is added as product. But the mechanism is inferred rather than experimentally resolved in discharge chemistry, the provenance of mol_009 is not justified in the graph, rxn_006 is still unbalanced and unquantified, and rxn_011 does not fully resolve a racemate to pure L-proline. This is a weak alternative entry, not a demonstrated pathway.', 'P6 has the same inferred Strecker logic as P5 and the same near-fixable balance issue in rxn_007, but it compounds that with the weaker final step rxn_012. The graph still lacks a justified source for mol_009, rxn_006 still needs explicit hydrolysis stoichiometry, and the chirality module is too speculative to claim target achievement. At best this is a conjectural route to some L-enrichment, not to validated L-proline.', 'P7 does not match current experimental support. The literature supports glutamate formation under some prebiotic hydrothermal conditions, but not glutamate to P5C or P5C to proline. rxn_008 is ground-truth UNBALANCED and omits the activation and reductant burden of carboxylate reduction; rxn_009 is UNBALANCED and needs H2 as reactant even before chemical plausibility is considered; rxn_011 still leaves chirality unresolved. This branch should be treated as a hypothesis, not a validated route.', 'P8 is weaker than P7 because it combines an unsupported hydrothermal proline branch with a highly speculative chirality module. rxn_008 remains an unbalanced and chemically uphill carboxylate reduction, rxn_009 still lacks the H2 needed even for stoichiometry, and rxn_012 does not provide a demonstrated prebiotic route from DL-proline to L-proline. This pathway is not currently viable.', 'P9 relies on the weakest literature support in the network. Hydrothermal arginine to proline is only a historical degradation report with uncertain product identification. rxn_010 is ground-truth UNBALANCED and omits required coproducts for the lost guanidino fragment; a coproduct such as guanidine or an ornithine-containing degradation network would need to be made explicit. Even if DL-proline were formed, rxn_011 still lacks a complete resolution mechanism to L-proline.', 'P10 combines the least supported synthetic branch with the least supported chirality step. rxn_010 is unbalanced and mechanistically unresolved, and rxn_012 is only a theoretical amplification idea here rather than a demonstrated proline-specific prebiotic process. This pathway does not currently substantiate production of L-proline.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-Proline\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The network is centered on the only experimentally resolved prebiotic route to the proline skeleton: the Patel-type cyanosulfidic surface sequence from HCN, acetylene, NH3, and sulfide. To correct the balance problems of earlier coarse renderings, the route is expanded into explicit aldehyde, cyanohydrin, cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide, thiolactam, imine, and aminonitrile nodes. Three chemically motivated choice points are encoded as parallel reactions: Cu-free vs Cu-accelerated aldehyde generation, Cu-free vs Cu-accelerated photodeoxygenation to the thiolactam, and late surface-photochemical vs hydrothermal H2/FeS/NiS desulfurization to the imine. No known prebiotic chemistry makes L-proline directly; therefore the target node is reached only after hydrolysis of proline aminonitrile and seed-dependent physical enantioenrichment, with the D-proline node used as the explicit non-target companion phase. Cyanamide diversion to arginine is acknowledged in reaction reasoning but omitted as a non-target sink.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethyne\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"prop-2-enenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-amino-2-hydroxybutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"proline-branch cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NOS\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-hydroxypyrrolidine-2-thione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NS\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"pyrrolidine-2-thione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"gamma-butyrothiolactam\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3,4-dihydro-2H-pyrrole\",\n      \"common_name\": \"1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"proline aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-proline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2R)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D-proline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu(I)-mediated HCN-acetylene coupling to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Post-impact surface streams or evaporitic pools; acetylene supplied by carbide hydrolysis; anoxic and UV-exposed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Copper(I)-promoted cyanovinylation of acetylene by HCN gives acrylonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. explicitly identify Cu(I)-catalyzed cross-coupling of HCN and acetylene to acrylonitrile as the entry to the proline branch (Patel et al., 2015). This is the best-supported carbon-skeleton entry, although Cu availability and acetylene delivery to surface waters remain geochemically contested (Ranjan et al., 2018; Todd et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Aza-Michael addition of ammonia to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich shallow pool or stream; near-neutral to mildly alkaline.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"NH3 adds conjugatively to the activated C=C bond of acrylonitrile to give beta-aminopropionitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. describe NH3 addition to acrylonitrile as the direct precursor step into the proline branch (Patel et al., 2015). The reaction is chemically reasonable without a catalyst in concentrated ammonia-bearing surface waters; cyanamide is omitted here because it diverts flux toward the arginine branch rather than the target.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Net UV-sulfidic reduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to 3-aminopanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated sulfidic pool; H2 is written as the net bookkeeping reductant for an atom-balanced representation of Patel UV/HS-/H2S photoreduction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net two-electron reduction of the nitrile to the aldehyde level with hydrolytic release of NH3.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. use UV/H2S-driven reductive homologation to move nitriles to the aldehyde level before HCN re-addition; here that bundled chemistry is separated into an explicit aldehyde-forming node (Patel et al., 2015). H2 is used only as a bookkeeping reductant for mass balance in the graph; experimentally the reducing equivalents come from sulfidic photochemistry rather than direct low-temperature hydrogenation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-photoredox-accelerated net reduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to 3-aminopanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox cycle\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated sulfidic pool; same net stoichiometry as rxn_003, but with Cu photoredox acceleration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoredox-accelerated net reduction of the nitrile to the aldehyde level with hydrolytic release of NH3.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the Cu-accelerated variant of the Patel aldehyde-generation step; Patel et al. state that Cu(I)-Cu(II) photoredox cycling accelerates the UV/H2S network (Patel et al., 2015). The chemistry is better supported kinetically than geochemically, because Cu-rich settings are patchier and more contested than sulfide availability alone (Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN addition to 3-aminopanal to form the proline-branch cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN-bearing pool; aldehyde capture should compete effectively at moderate HCN activity.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The amino aldehyde undergoes Strecker-type cyanohydrin formation by HCN addition.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. place HCN addition after reduction to the amino aldehyde, giving the cyanohydrin that feeds sulfur incorporation (Patel et al., 2015). Writing this step explicitly avoids the earlier unbalanced bundling and makes clear where the extra carbon of the proline skeleton enters.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"H2S-driven thioamidation and cyclization to the cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Sulfidic surface water; intramolecular ring closure accompanies sulfur incorporation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrosulfide converts the nitrile to a thioamide, followed by intramolecular cyclization to the five-membered cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide with NH3 loss.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. report alpha-hydroxythioamides from the cyanohydrin plus H2S and note that the cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide forms in the proline branch (Patel et al., 2015). The cyclic product is the chemically relevant precursor because it feeds directly into gamma-butyrothiolactam formation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Net photodeoxygenation of the cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide to gamma-butyrothiolactam\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-exposed sulfidic pool; H2 again represents the net reducing equivalents for an atom-balanced graph representation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net reductive deoxygenation of the alpha-hydroxyl group gives the cyclic thioamide gamma-butyrothiolactam.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. identify gamma-butyrothiolactam as the only deoxygenated thioamide from this branch (Patel et al., 2015). As in rxn_003, the explicit H2 bookkeeping is used to keep the network balanced while preserving the experimentally motivated UV/sulfide redox logic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-photoredox-accelerated net photodeoxygenation to gamma-butyrothiolactam\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox cycle\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated sulfidic pool; same net stoichiometry as rxn_007, with Cu acceleration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoredox-accelerated net deoxygenation of the cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide to the thiolactam.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the Cu-accelerated counterpart of rxn_007, justified by Patel et al.'s broader observation that Cu(I)-Cu(II) cycling accelerates the UV/H2S reductive network (Patel et al., 2015). It remains surface-plausible only in locally Cu-enriched niches, a limitation emphasized by geochemical critiques (Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface reductive desulfurization of gamma-butyrothiolactam to 1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Net-balanced representation of the further Patel photoreduction; the 1-pyrroline intermediate is inferred rather than isolated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive desulfurization/hydrogenolysis of the thiolactam generates the cyclic imine 1-pyrroline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. report conversion of gamma-butyrothiolactam to proline aminonitrile over two further steps, with the cyclic imine best interpreted as an inferred intermediate (Patel et al., 2015). This explicit desulfurization node is therefore mechanistically motivated and improves balance, but it should be read as an inferred decomposition of the published sequence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal FeS-NiS-assisted reductive desulfurization of gamma-butyrothiolactam to 1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\", \"NiS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS/NiS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"H2-rich alkaline vent or subseafloor reactor; speculative late-stage handoff from a surface-generated thiolactam pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal-sulfide-assisted hydrogenolytic desulfurization of the thiolactam to the cyclic imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This branch is speculative: no prebiotic gamma-butyrothiolactam to 1-pyrroline reduction has been demonstrated. It is included because H2 with Ni- and FeS-rich systems reduces related carbonyl and imine chemistry in protometabolic work, making this a chemically plausible but untested late-stage alternative (Huber and Wachtershauser, 2003; Kitadai et al., 2019; Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN capture of 1-pyrroline to proline aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN-rich surface pool; imine trapping favored before hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Strecker-type addition of HCN to the cyclic iminium/imine manifold gives pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"HCN capture of the cyclic imine completes the proline aminonitrile skeleton; Patel et al. report 32% overall for the last two steps from gamma-butyrothiolactam to pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile, with the imine intermediate inferred (Patel et al., 2015). Ritson later emphasized that the cyclic proline aminonitrile is unusually resistant to retro-Strecker reversion compared with many acyclic aminonitriles, making it a plausible accumulation point (Ritson, 2021).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of proline aminonitrile with seed-dependent eutectic partition to L- and D-proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Slow nitrile hydrolysis during wet-dry or geothermal cycles, followed by evaporation/crystallization or eutectic partitioning in concentrated prebiotic pools; requires a small initial L-bias from an unresolved source. mol_016 is a bookkeeping stand-in for the non-target D- or DL-rich companion phase.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminonitrile hydrolysis proceeds through the usual amide/carboxyl sequence to proline; subsequent phase-behavior separation enriches one enantiomer in the recoverable solution fraction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. stop at proline aminonitrile and do not report a hydrolysis yield to proline, so the hydrolysis half of this node is chemically necessary but experimentally unquantified in the cited cyanosulfidic system (Patel et al., 2015). The L-specific outcome is not direct asymmetric synthesis: it represents hydrolysis followed by eutectic/phase-behavior resolution, for which proline is unusually well documented, although aqueous/alcohol-relevant systems still need a seed ee and the strongest CHCl3 case is not prebiotic (Klussmann et al., 2006; Blackmond, 2019).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Baseline surface cyanosulfidic route\",\n      \"description\": \"All redox nodes follow the non-Cu surface-photoredox representations before HCN trapping and seed-dependent recovery of an L-proline fraction.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted aldehyde-release variant\",\n      \"description\": \"Cu photoredox is invoked only for the nitrile-to-aldehyde step; later thiolactam formation and imine release remain on the baseline surface route.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted thiolactam-forming variant\",\n      \"description\": \"Only the alpha-hydroxythioamide-to-thiolactam conversion is assigned to the Cu-accelerated photoredox branch.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Fully Cu-assisted surface photoredox variant\",\n      \"description\": \"Both Patel-type photoredox reductions are assigned to Cu-accelerated versions while the late desulfurization remains a surface completion step.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface-to-hydrothermal completion route\",\n      \"description\": \"Surface cyanosulfidic construction is followed by a speculative hydrothermal H2/FeS/NiS desulfurization of gamma-butyrothiolactam before HCN trapping and hydrolysis/resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted aldehyde generation with hydrothermal completion\",\n      \"description\": \"The early nitrile-to-aldehyde step is Cu-accelerated, but the late thiolactam-to-imine conversion is shifted to the speculative hydrothermal H2/FeS/NiS branch.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted thiolactam formation with hydrothermal completion\",\n      \"description\": \"Cu acceleration is used only at the alpha-hydroxythioamide deoxygenation step, followed by hydrothermal desulfurization to the imine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Fully Cu-assisted mixed-environment route\",\n      \"description\": 