2015). Ritson later emphasized that the cyclic proline aminonitrile is unusually resistant to retro-Strecker reversion compared with many acyclic aminonitriles, making it a plausible accumulation point (Ritson, 2021)."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Hydrolysis of proline aminonitrile with seed-dependent eutectic partition to L- and D-proline", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_003"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Slow nitrile hydrolysis during wet-dry or geothermal cycles, followed by evaporation/crystallization or eutectic partitioning in concentrated prebiotic pools; requires a small initial L-bias from an unresolved source. mol_016 is a bookkeeping stand-in for the non-target D- or DL-rich companion phase."}, "mechanism": "Aminonitrile hydrolysis proceeds through the usual amide/carboxyl sequence to proline; subsequent phase-behavior separation enriches one enantiomer in the recoverable solution fraction.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. stop at proline aminonitrile and do not report a hydrolysis yield to proline, so the hydrolysis half of this node is chemically necessary but experimentally unquantified in the cited cyanosulfidic system (Patel et al., 2015). The L-specific outcome is not direct asymmetric synthesis: it represents hydrolysis followed by eutectic/phase-behavior resolution, for which proline is unusually well documented, although aqueous/alcohol-relevant systems still need a seed ee and the strongest CHCl3 case is not prebiotic (Klussmann et al., 2006; Blackmond, 2019)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Baseline surface cyanosulfidic route", "description": "All redox nodes follow the non-Cu surface-photoredox representations before HCN trapping and seed-dependent recovery of an L-proline fraction.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_0