action as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.44\n- Feedback: The network is well framed around the correct literature backbone: the Patel cyanosulfidic route is rightly central, and it is good that meteorite and ice detections were not promoted to mechanistic terrestrial pathways. The graph is connected and uses consistent molecule IDs. However network validity is substantially weakened because 8 of 12 reaction nodes are ground-truth UNBALANCED: rxn_003, rxn_004, rxn_005, rxn_006, rxn_007, rxn_008, rxn_009, and rxn_010. Specific fixes are available for some nodes: rxn_007 needs H2O as product; rxn_009 needs H2 as reactant; rxn_006 should be written as mol_008 + 2 H2O -> mol_013 + NH3; rxn_010 needs explicit coproducts for the lost guanidino fragment, with guanidine being one plausible net coproduct. rxn_005 may need sulfur coproducts or H2S moved from reactants to conditions; a possible shorthand is mol_007 + mol_001 -> mol_008 + S. rxn_003 and rxn_004 cannot be rescued by a simple small-species patch and should be expanded into Patel's explicit aldehyde, cyanohydrin, thioamide, thiolactam, and imine-level substeps. rxn_008 also needs explicit activation and reductant chemistry, not just a one-arrow hydrothermal conversion. The hydrothermal glutamate or P5C and arginine branches are much less supported than the cyanosulfidic branch and should be marked as hypotheses rather than parallel validated routes. Finally, rxn_011 and especially rxn_012 are not full synthetic conversions to L-proline; proline phase behavior can enrich ee only with a seed ee, explicit solvent or eutectic conditions, and a modeled D-enriched companion phase, and the Biochemical environment label should be replaced by explicit prebiotic pool, eutectic, freeze-thaw, or evaporation-crystallization settings. Overall, the network is coherent in concept and literature-aware, but it is not currently a chemically valid or fully target-achieving synthesis network for L-proline.\n- Issues: ['P1 is the strongest and most literature-aligned branch because rxn_001 and rxn_002 match the Patel cyanosulfidic entry to the proline skeleton. The explicit network then breaks conservation at the core: rxn_003 and rxn_005 are ground-truth UNBALANCED bundled transformations, and rxn_006 is UNBALANCED and should include 2 H2O as reactants and NH3 as product. Literature support is also strongest only up to mol_008; the aminonitrile hydrolysis to proline is not quantified here. rxn_011 reflects real proline phase behavior, but it is a resolution step rather than a full DL to L conversion and needs a seed ee plus an explicit D-rich companion phase. Good route to proline aminonitrile; not yet a validated route to isolated L-proline.', 'P2 keeps the same credible early cyanosulfidic backbone as P1, but rxn_003, rxn_005, and rxn_006 remain unbalanced and therefore invalid as written. The final step is weaker than in P1: rxn_012 is a speculative sequential amplification module rather than an experimentally established prebiotic resolution of proline. As drawn, this path does not convincingly deliver L-proline beyond a hypothetical enrichment scenario.', 'P3 follows the Cu-assisted Patel variant and is reasonable on stated conditions, but Cu acceleration does not fix the reaction specification. rxn_004 and rxn_005 are ground-truth UNBALANCED bundled nets, rxn_006 should include 2 H2O and NH3, and the literature support still weakens after mol_008 because hydrolysis to proline is unquantified. rxn_011 again represents enrichment or resolution behavior, not a complete synthetic conversion to L-proline. This remains a partial route to racemic proline precursors rather than a validated route to L-proline.', 'P4 inherits the same chemically sensible entry from Patel chemistry as P3, but the explicit core still violates conservation in rxn_004 and rxn_005, and rxn_006 still omits the water and ammonia required for hydrolysis. The final rxn_012 step is even less secure than rxn_011 because sequential phase-behavior amplification is not demonstrated here for proline under prebiotic conditions. The route is coherent as a hypothesis, not as a validated synthesis to L-proline.', 'P5 is chemically conceivable as an inferred Strecker capture of 4-aminobutanal or 1-pyrroline, and rxn_007 is close to correct if H2O is added as product. But the mechanism is inferred rather than experimentally resolved in discharge chemistry, the provenance of mol_009 is not justified in the graph, rxn_006 is still unbalanced and unquantified, and rxn_011 does not fully resolve a racemate to pure L-proline. This is a weak alternative entry, not a demonstrated pathway.', 'P6 has the same inferred Strecker logic as P5 and the same near-fixable balance issue in rxn_007, but it compounds that with the weaker final step rxn_012. The graph still lacks a justified source for mol_009, rxn_006 still needs explicit hydrolysis stoichiometry, and the chirality module is too speculative to claim target achievement. At best this is a conjectural route to some L-enrichment, not to validated L-proline.', 'P7 does not match current experimental support. The literature supports glutamate formation under some prebiotic hydrothermal conditions, but not glutamate to P5C or P5C to proline. rxn_008 is ground-truth UNBALANCED and omits the activation and reductant burden of carboxylate reduction; rxn_009 is UNBALANCED and needs H2 as reactant even before chemical plausibility is considered; rxn_011 still leaves chirality unresolved. This branch should be treated as a hypothesis, not a validated route.', 'P8 is weaker than P7 because it combines an unsupported hydrothermal proline branch with a highly speculative chirality module. rxn_008 remains an unbalanced and chemically uphill carboxylate reduction, rxn_009 still lacks the H2 needed even for stoichiometry, and rxn_012 does not provide a demonstrated prebiotic route from DL-proline to L-proline. This pathway is not currently viable.', 'P9 relies on the weakest literature support in the network. Hydrothermal arginine to proline is only a historical degradation report with uncertain product identification. rxn_010 is ground-truth UNBALANCED and omits required coproducts for the lost guanidino fragment; a coproduct such as guanidine or an ornithine-containing degradation network would need to be made explicit. Even if DL-proline were formed, rxn_011 still lacks a complete resolution mechanism to L-proline.', 'P10 combines the least supported synthetic branch with the least supported chirality step. rxn_010 is unbalanced and mechanistically unresolved, and rxn_012 is only a theoretical amplification idea here rather than a demonstrated proline-specific prebiotic process. This pathway does not currently substantiate production of L-proline.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.75\n- Feedback: Network validity is strong: the graph is connected, molecule IDs are consistent, and the RDKit check reports 0 UNBALANCED reactions. rxn_012 is not a conservation violation but is only balanceable with coefficients and should be rewritten as 2 mol_014 + 4 mol_005 -> mol_015 + mol_016 + 2 mol_003 or split into separate hydrolysis and resolution steps. Literature alignment is good through proline aminonitrile: the explicit acrylonitrile, beta-aminopropionitrile, aldehyde, cyanohydrin, cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide, gamma-butyrothiolactam, and inferred 1-pyrroline nodes match the Patel-type cyanosulfidic backbone and correctly acknowledge that hydrolysis to proline is unquantified. The main deficits are chemical and environmental: rxn_003/004, rxn_007/008, and rxn_009/010 use H2 bookkeeping instead of explicitly encoding the cited UV/HS-/H2S redox chemistry; the Cu-accelerated variants are distinguished mostly by annotation rather than explicit catalyst metadata; rxn_010 is a speculative hydrothermal extrapolation; mixed routes require unmodeled transfer of reactive intermediates between surface and hydrothermal settings; and rxn_012 conflates chemical hydrolysis with physical enantioenrichment in a mislabeled biochemical environment. P9 and P10 also differ from P4 and P8 mostly by pathway label because the seed sources are not explicit nodes or conditions. The network therefore reaches L-proline only partially and much more convincingly reaches proline aminonitrile; if the target were proline aminonitrile rather than L-proline, confidence would be substantially higher. Best current variant is P9, but the complete L-proline network remains below the approval threshold. Consider making sulfur redox and chiral-resolution steps explicit and, if mol_013 remains only inferred, combining rxn_009 and rxn_011 into a single literature-backed late transformation with an annotated imine intermediate.\n- Issues: ['Strong Patel-style surface backbone through mol_014 and good literature alignment for the proline skeleton. The main limitations are that rxn_003 and rxn_007 are encoded as H2-driven net reductions instead of explicit UV/HS-/H2S photoredox steps, rxn_009 makes the inferred 1-pyrroline intermediate a standalone surface reaction, and rxn_012 combines hydrolysis with chiral partition rather than representing them separately.', 'Cu-assisted aldehyde release is consistent with the Patel framework and slightly improves the early reduction logic. Confidence is still capped by the H2 shorthand in rxn_004 and rxn_007, the inferred/desulfurization character of rxn_009, and the fact that rxn_012 only reaches L-proline as one member of a racemic or partitioned output.', 'Cu-accelerated photodeoxygenation is literature-motivated and the sequence remains coherent to mol_014. As in P1, the net reductive bookkeeping in rxn_003 and rxn_008 is under-specified, rxn_009 is inferred rather than directly demonstrated, and rxn_012 should not merge hydrolysis with enantioenrichment.', 'Best purely chemical surface backbone: the fully Cu-assisted Patel sequence is coherent and literature-aligned through proline aminonitrile. The remaining weaknesses are the H2 shorthand in rxn_004 and rxn_008, the inferred standalone desulfurization of rxn_009, and the under-specified final L/D separation in rxn_012.', 'The hydrothermal diversion is the dominant weakness. rxn_010 is a speculative FeS/NiS/H2 extrapolation, and the pathway then requires surface to hydrothermal to surface handling of late intermediates, with mol_013 needing to encounter HCN again in surface rxn_011 without an explicit transport justification; rxn_012 remains the same weak final step.', 'Cu-assisted aldehyde generation is reasonable, but the late-stage logic is limited by the same mixed-environment problems as P5. The hydrothermal rxn_010 is weakly supported for proline, the return of mol_013 to a surface HCN pool for rxn_011 is not encoded, and rxn_012 still does not provide a clearly prebiotic route to L-proline.', 'The early surface chemistry is plausible, but the hydrothermal completion remains poorly supported. rxn_010 is speculative, the hydrothermal-to-surface handoff before rxn_011 is not justified for a reactive 1-pyrroline intermediate, and rxn_012 still conflates hydrolysis with physical chiral enrichment.', 'Chemically ambitious but the weakest late-stage literature alignment: it combines full Cu-assisted surface photoredox with a speculative hydrothermal desulfurization. The required surface to hydrothermal to surface transfers are not modeled, and the L-proline endpoint still depends on the under-specified rxn_012.', 'Best target-oriented variant: it preserves the literature-backed fully Cu-assisted surface backbone and at least names a plausible asymmetry source. However the CPL seed is only in the pathway label rather than an explicit node or condition set, proline-specific CPL enantioenrichment is indirect in the literature, and rxn_012 still represents racemic hydrolysis or partition more than true L-selective synthesis.', 'Chiral sugar or mineral seeding improves the target logic, but the seed is not explicitly represented in the graph and the mixed surface/hydrothermal completion remains speculative. As in other mixed routes, rxn_010 and the unmodeled transfer into surface rxn_011 weaken the late stage, while rxn_012 should be separated into chemical hydrolysis and physical resolution.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-Proline\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"On chemical merit, the network is built around the only intermediate-resolved prebiotic route to the proline skeleton: Patel-style surface cyanosulfidic chemistry. Hydrothermal glutamate/ornithine ideas and discharge or ice detections are not expanded into productive branches because the supplied literature treats their proline-forming steps as speculative or detection-only. Ten distinct pathways are realized as combinatorial variants at four experimentally motivated branch points within the Patel backbone: sulfide-only vs Cu-accelerated nitrile reduction, sulfide-only vs Cu-accelerated photodeoxygenation, sulfide-only vs Cu-accelerated late desulfurative HCN capture, and two aminonitrile hydrolysis regimes. All chemical branches first yield racemic proline; a separate final phase-behavior node resolves or amplifies that pool toward L-proline, because no abiotic route is known to make L-proline directly. Free cyanohydrin and inferred 1-pyrroline are treated as mechanistic transients inside atom-balanced nodes so sulfur redox stays explicit and hydrolysis remains separated from chiral amplification.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n     