,(H,7,8)/t4+/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu(I)-catalyzed HCN-acetylene coupling to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic post-impact or evaporitic surface pool; acetylene plausibly supplied by carbide hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu(I)-mediated cyanovinylation of acetylene by HCN.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. identify Cu(I)-catalyzed cross-coupling of HCN and acetylene as the entry to the proline and arginine branch, giving acrylonitrile (Patel et al., 2015). This is the best-supported prebiotic start to the proline skeleton, although its geochemical plausibility depends on local Cu availability and on acetylene release from impact-derived carbides; those constraints are emphasized by Ranjan et al. (2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Aza-Michael addition of ammonia to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"NH3-rich local pulse in anoxic water.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Conjugate aza-Michael addition across the activated alkene.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ammonia addition to acrylonitrile to give beta-aminopropionitrile is explicitly discussed as a proline precursor in the cyanosulfidic network (Patel et al., 2015). The aza-Michael addition is chemically straightforward in water and does not require a separate catalyst.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"UV/H2S reduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation in sulfide-bearing, mildly alkaline water; laboratory analogs used 254 nm-equivalent light.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoredox reduction of the nitrile to an imidoyl or iminium level followed by hydrolytic release of the aldehyde, with sulfide oxidized to elemental sulfur.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. use UV/H2S photoredox to reduce nitriles to aldehydes within the cyanosulfidic network (Patel et al., 2015). This node writes that chemistry as the atom-balanced net beta-aminopropionitrile + H2O + H2S -> 3-aminopropanal + NH3 + S; the detailed radical sequence is abbreviated, but sulfur redox is explicit. Surface sulfide availability remains a known environmental limitation (Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-photoredox UV/H2S reduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox cycle\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation in sulfide-bearing water with locally available Cu; laboratory analogs used 254 nm-equivalent light.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same photoredox nitrile-to-aldehyde conversion as rxn_003, accelerated by Cu(I)/Cu(II) cycling.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Same chemical destination as rxn_003, but with Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox acceleration, which Patel et al. report can speed UV/H2S reductions without changing the overall transformation (Patel et al., 2015). This variant is useful where local Cu is available, but it inherits the same Cu-abundance and staged-delivery critiques discussed by Ranjan et al. (2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN capture and cyclothioamidation to the cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Transient cyanohydrin formation and intramolecular ring closure occur in the same HCN and H2S pulse.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldehyde capture by HCN to a cyanohydrin, sulfur insertion or thioamidation of the nitrile, then intramolecular cyclization to the observed cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. report aldehyde capture by HCN followed by sulfur chemistry to alpha-hydroxythioamides, including a cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide on the proline branch (Patel et al., 2015). For compactness, the free cyanohydrin is treated here as a mechanistic transient inside the atom-balanced node rather than as a separate graph node.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"UV/H2S photodeoxygenation of the cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide to gamma-butyrothiolactam\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation with sulfide acting as stoichiometric reductant in anoxic surface water.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photodeoxygenation of the alpha-hydroxythioamide to the cyclic thiolactam with explicit sulfur oxidation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Photodeoxygenation of the cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide gives gamma-butyrothiolactam as the only deoxygenated thioamide observed on this branch (Patel et al., 2015). The balanced net uses H2S as reductant and writes elemental sulfur plus water explicitly rather than hiding the redox chemistry in abstract H2 bookkeeping.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-photoredox UV/H2S photodeoxygenation of the cyclic alpha-hydroxythioamide to gamma-butyrothiolactam\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox cycle\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation in sulfide-bearing water with local Cu available for photoredox cycling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same photodeoxygenation as rxn_006, accelerated by Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox can accelerate the same UV/H2S photodeoxygenation chemistry (Patel et al., 2015). This alternative keeps the same stoichiometric outputs as rxn_006 but represents a more Cu-rich local surface microenvironment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Photodesulfurization and HCN capture of gamma-butyrothiolactam\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\