l observation that Cu(I)/Cu(II) cycling speeds cyanosulfidic reductions (Patel et al., 2015). The imine-trapping logic is the same; only the local catalytic regime differs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Aqueous hydrolysis of proline aminonitrile to DL-proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Long residence time in standing water or geothermal pools; racemic product expected.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Stepwise nitrile hydration via the amide to the carboxylic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrolysis of pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile to proline is chemically standard and is the necessary completion of the Patel branch, but Patel et al. did not report a proline yield from the aminonitrile (Patel et al., 2015). This node is therefore plausible yet unquantified for proline specifically. Ritson (2021) supports accumulation of the proline aminonitrile because, unlike many acyclic aminonitriles, it resists reversion to the cyanohydrin.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Wet-dry mineral-assisted hydrolysis of proline aminonitrile to DL-proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\", \"phosphate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite / phosphate minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Evaporation-rehydration on clay and phosphate-rich shores; racemic product expected.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Heterogeneous hydration and hydrolysis of the aminonitrile under wet-dry concentration cycles.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative alternative to rxn_010: wet-dry concentration on clay or phosphate-rich shores could accelerate the same aminonitrile-to-amino-acid hydrolysis by increasing local concentration and residence time. No proline-specific prebiotic yield is reported in the provided literature, so this node is explicitly marked speculative and is included only as a chemically plausible surface completion step (Patel et al., 2015; Ritson, 2021).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Eutectic phase amplification and resolution of DL-proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"273-300K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Freeze-thaw or evaporation-crystallization in concentrated brine; requires a tiny prior L-ee and is represented schematically as 2 DL-proline -> L-proline + D-proline.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Solution-solid partitioning of a racemic compound or eutectic, enriching the mobile phase in one enantiomer and the complementary phase in the other.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No abiotic route in the provided literature makes L-proline directly. Proline is nevertheless unusually favorable for physical ee amplification: its solution composition is constrained by eutectic phase behavior, with 54-58% ee measured in MeOH and EtOH and far stronger but non-prebiotic CHCl3 cosolvate behavior (Klussmann et al., 2006). This node abstracts conservative prebiotic freeze-thaw or evaporation partitioning of a slightly L-biased DL pool into L-proline and a complementary D-rich phase; it is a resolution or amplification step, not an asymmetric synthesis. A prior tiny L bias is required and could in principle come from CPL or other symmetry-breaking processes, but it is not generated here (Modica et al., 2014).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Baseline downstream sulfide-only branch with aqueous hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Cu is used only in the entry coupling; all downstream photoredox steps are sulfide-only, the aminonitrile hydrolyzes in standing water, and the resulting DL-proline is phase-resolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Baseline downstream sulfide-only branch with mineral hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The same downstream sulfide-only photoredox sequence as P1, but aminonitrile hydrolysis is assigned to wet-dry mineral surfaces before chiral resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Late Cu-assisted capture branch\",\n      \"description\": \"Early downstream steps remain sulfide-only, but the final thiolactam to aminonitrile conversion uses Cu-assisted photoredox before aqueous hydrolysis and resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted deoxygenation only\",\n      \"description\": \"Aldehyde release is sulfide-only, photodeoxygenation to the thiolactam is Cu-accelerated, and the late aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis follow 