"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["montmorillonite", "phosphate minerals"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "montmorillonite / phosphate minerals", "aqueous": true, "other": "Evaporation-rehydration on clay and phosphate-rich shores; racemic product expected."}, "mechanism": "Heterogeneous hydration and hydrolysis of the aminonitrile under wet-dry concentration cycles.", "reasoning": "Speculative alternative to rxn_010: wet-dry concentration on clay or phosphate-rich shores could accelerate the same aminonitrile-to-amino-acid hydrolysis by increasing local concentration and residence time. No proline-specific prebiotic yield is reported in the provided literature, so this node is explicitly marked speculative and is included only as a chemically plausible surface completion step (Patel et al., 2015; Ritson, 2021)."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Eutectic phase amplification and resolution of DL-proline", "inputs": ["mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_015"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "273-300K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Freeze-thaw or evaporation-crystallization in concentrated brine; requires a tiny prior L-ee and is represented schematically as 2 DL-proline -> L-proline + D-proline."}, "mechanism": "Solution-solid partitioning of a racemic compound or eutectic, enriching the mobile phase in one enantiomer and the complementary phase in the other.", "reasoning": "No abiotic route in the provided literature makes L-proline directly. Proline is nevertheless unusually favorable for physical ee amplification: its solution composition is constrained by eutectic phase behavior, with 54-58% ee measured in MeOH and EtOH and far stronger but non-prebiotic CHCl3 cosolvate behavior (Klussmann et al., 2006). This node abstracts conservative prebiotic freeze-thaw or evaporation partitioning of a slightly